# 3. Model Suite Architecture & Mathematical Formulations

We evaluate 8 baseline algorithms. These span standard MARL paradigms, counterfactual advantage methods, dynamic Bayesian causal graphs, and treatment effect contrastive learning.

## 3.1 Model 1: IPPO (Independent PPO)

IPPO treats each agent $ i \in \mathcal{N} $ as an independent learning agent. It ignores non-stationarity. The policy network $ \pi_{\theta_i}(a_i|o_i) $ maps local obs $ o_i \in \mathbb{R}^{53} $ to action logits over $ \mathcal{A}_i $. The value network $ V_{\phi_i}(o_i) $ acts as a local critic. It estimates state value from $ o_i $.

The PPO clipped objective is:

$ L_i^{\text{CLIP}}(\theta_i) = \hat{\mathbb{E}}_t \left[ \min \left( r_{t,i}(\theta_i) \hat{A}_{t,i}, \text{clip}(r_{t,i}(\theta_i), 1-\epsilon, 1+\epsilon) \hat{A}_{t,i} \right) \right] $

We calculate $ r_{t,i}(\theta_i) = \frac{\pi_{\theta_i}(a_{t,i}|o_{t,i})}{\pi_{\theta_i}^{\text{old}}(a_{t,i}|o_{t,i})} $. We compute $ \hat{A}_{t,i} $ via GAE($ \lambda = 0.95 $).

---

## 3.2 Model 2: MAPPO (Multi-Agent PPO with Centralized Critic)

MAPPO replaces individual local critics with a centralized critic $ V_{\Phi}(s_t) $. It consumes the global state $ s_t $. The joint policy decouples actors $ \pi_{\theta_i}(a_i|o_i) $ for decentralized execution.

We define centralized advantage: $ \hat{A}_t^{\text{central}} = \sum_{l=0}^{\infty} (\gamma \lambda)^l \delta_{t+l}^{V} $. The temporal difference error is $ \delta_t^V = r_t + \gamma V_{\Phi}(s_{t+1}) - V_{\Phi}(s_t) $.

---

## 3.3 Model 3: COMA (Counterfactual Multi-Agent Policy Gradients)

COMA computes a counterfactual advantage baseline for agent $ i $ using a centralized critic $ Q(s, \mathbf{a}) $:

$ A_i^{\text{COMA}}(s, \mathbf{a}) = Q(s, \mathbf{a}) - \sum_{a_i' \in \mathcal{A}_i} \pi_i(a_i' | o_i) Q(s, (a_i', \mathbf{a}_{-i})) $

This marginalizes out agent $ i $'s action. Teammate actions $ \mathbf{a}_{-i} $ remain fixed.

---

## 3.4 Model 4: MAPPO + CAR (Causal Affordance Credit)

MAPPO_CAR injects action-mask affordance delta feedback into agent rewards:

$ r_{t,i}^{\text{CAR}} = r_{t,i} + \beta_{\text{CAR}} \sum_{j \neq i} \Delta \text{Mask}_j(s_t, a_{t,i}) $

The function $ \Delta \text{Mask}_j(s_t, a_{t,i}) = |\text{Mask}_j(s_{t+1})| - |\text{Mask}_j(s_t)| $ measures how agent $ i $'s action expanded available action affordances for teammate $ j $.

---

## 3.5 Model 5: MAPPO + CIR (Causal Advantage Routing)

MAPPO_CIR routes advantage estimation based on causal contribution. It does not use uniform spatial sharing:

$ \hat{A}_{t,i}^{\text{CIR}} = \hat{A}_t^{\text{central}} \cdot \psi_i(s_t, \mathbf{a}_t) $

The routing function $ \psi_i(s_t, \mathbf{a}_t) = \frac{\exp(\| \nabla_{a_i} Q(s_t, \mathbf{a}_t) \|)}{\sum_{j} \exp(\| \nabla_{a_j} Q(s_t, \mathbf{a}_t) \|)} $ measures the local Jacobian sensitivity of the joint state-value.

---

## 3.6 Model 6: LOO (Leave-One-Out Marginal Counterfactual)

LOO implements a Leave-One-Out marginal counterfactual advantage:

$ A_i^{\text{LOO}}(s, \mathbf{a}) = Q_i(s, a_{-i}, a_i) - \frac{1}{|\mathcal{A}_{\text{legal}}|-1} \sum_{a' \neq a_i} Q_i(s, a_{-i}, a') $

LOO isolates the marginal contribution without taking expectations over uncalibrated policy distributions $ \pi_i(a_i'|o_i) $. It reduces counterfactual variance compared to COMA.

---

## 3.7 Model 7: ATE (Average Treatment Effect Contrastive PPO)

ATE measures the direct treatment effect of executing an action $ a_i $. It compares it to the explicit null action ($ a_{\text{WAIT}} = 4 $):

$ A_i^{\text{ATE}}(s, a_i) = Q_i(s, a_{-i}, a_i) - Q_i(s, a_{-i}, a_{\text{WAIT}}) $

---

## 3.8 Model 8: MACCA (Multi-Agent Dynamic Bayesian Causal Graph)

MACCA factorizes global state transitions into a Dynamic Bayesian Network (DBN). It measures inter-agent influence graphs:

$ \hat{A}_{t,i}^{\text{MACCA}} = \hat{A}_{t,i}^{\text{central}} \cdot \left[ 1 + \eta_{\text{MACCA}} \sum_{j \neq i} P_{\text{DBN}}(s_{j, t+1} \mid s_t, a_{i, t}) \right] $

---

## 3.9 Model 9: MARC (Micro-Macro Asymmetric Retroactive Causal-chain)

MARC factorizes credit assignment into a multi-scale Micro-Macro structure.

Micro Credit ($ \mu_{i, t} $) evaluates local interaction impact via action affordance deltas:

$ \mu_{i, t} = \hat{A}_{i, t}^{\text{base}} + \beta_{\text{affordance}} \sum_{j \neq i} \Delta \text{Mask}_j(s_t, a_{i, t}) $

Macro Weighting ($ \Omega_t $) scales micro credit by global alarm ($ A_t $) and team outcome ($ R_T $):

$ \Omega_t = \Phi_{\text{outcome}}(R_T) \cdot \exp\left( -\alpha_{\text{alarm}} \frac{A_t}{A_{\text{max}}} \right) $

Asymmetric Failure Shielding protects enablers. On team failure, negative credit targets direct failure triggers. It shields upstream enabling actions ($ \Omega_t = 1.0 $ for enablers).

The Backward Retroactive Pass ($ t = T \to 0 $) propagates joint Micro-Macro advantages backward through time:

$ \hat{A}_{i, t}^{\text{MARC}} = \mu_{i, t} \cdot \Omega_t + \gamma_{\text{causal}} \hat{A}_{i, t+1}^{\text{MARC}} $

### 3.9.1 Targeted Component Ablations
We evaluate three ablations for MARC.
The `marc_no_shielding` ablation disables enabler shielding on team failure. It applies negative outcome penalties to all agents.
The `marc_no_macro` ablation sets $ \Omega_t = 1.0 $. It removes global alarm penalty scaling and outcome factor weighting.
The `marc_no_affordance` ablation sets $ \beta_{\text{affordance}} = 0.0 $. It disables action-mask expansion feedback.

## 3.10 Hierarchical MARL (H-MARL) Solutions to the Sparsity Wall

We introduce CHARM (Causal Hierarchical Asymmetric Routing & Messaging) alongside three canonical H-MARL baselines. These architectures utilize a Manager operating at a macro-step frequency $ c $ and a Worker operating at the environment frequency.

### 3.10.1 CHARM (Causal Hierarchical Asymmetric Routing & Messaging)
CHARM's Manager outputs a continuous spatial goal $ g \in \mathbb{R}^2 $. The Worker learns from the sparse extrinsic reward $ R_{ext} $. We inject dense upward affordance routing to the Manager:
$$$
R_{Manager} = R_{ext} + \lambda \cdot R_{affordance}
$$$
This prevents causal cycle traps. It densifies the macro-gradient.

### 3.10.2 MAHIRO (Multi-Agent HIRO)
MAHIRO acts as our continuous-goal baseline. The Manager outputs $ g \in \mathbb{R}^2 $. The Worker receives an intrinsic reward proportional to the negative L2 distance to the goal:
$$$
r^{in}_t = -|| s_{t} - g_c ||_2
$$$
The Manager receives only the raw extrinsic reward $ R_{ext} $.

### 3.10.3 ROMA (Role-Oriented Multi-Agent RL)
ROMA utilizes a discrete Manager that outputs a latent role $ z $. The Worker policy $ \pi(a | \tau, z) $ depends on this role. A KL-divergence regularizer enforces role diversity:
$$$
L_{ROMA} = L_{PPO} + \alpha_{KL} \sum_{i,j} D_{KL}( \pi( \cdot | \tau_i, z_i ) || \pi( \cdot | \tau_j, z_j ) )
$$$

### 3.10.4 LRS (Logical Reward Shaping)
LRS structurally matches ROMA by outputting a discrete state phase $ \phi $. The Worker is rewarded for transitioning the global state to satisfy the logical preconditions of $ \phi $. It frames the problem as a progression through a Finite State Automaton.
